A||||5||Cheever3000||Going to trailer my car - how's best to tie it down?||||2||
Z||000000||Cheever3000||08-29-2001||03:10 PM||||My Coupe's insurance & registration expired but I want to take it to some shows soon.  I don't know what it takes to trailer it.  Surely I don't want to just put the e-brake on and nothing else, right?  Aren't some straps necessary?  What do you other trailer trash  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> folks do?  This is a nice big trailer with two axles and about a foot high railing around the sides & front (nothing across the back).<P>||||reg||2
Z||000001||Ken Wittlief||08-29-2001||03:14 PM||||goto walmart and get 4 nylon straps with adjustable lockdowns. When you strap the car down, dont have the straps running straight up from the trailer to the car - you want them at 45° angles - the two in back will hold the car in place when you are braking, the two in front 'pull the car forward' (with the trailer) when you are accelerating.<P> and all 4 hold it down going over bumps.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cheever3000||08-29-2001||03:18 PM||||Putting them over the body of the car, or over the tires, or what? (forgive my ignorance, I just never did this before)<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||08-29-2001||03:34 PM||||I made my own hauler. I use galvanized chain with the screw together links. Loop it over lower a arms to trailer frame. Lets the car still bounce on its suspension without it moving. I never did trust nylon straps myself.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Wolfhound||08-29-2001||03:38 PM||||Put it on the trailor backward. It will tow better weight forward. Attach to the cradle and anywhere on the front suspension. Anti sway bar is a good point. Use emergency brake and have it in gear also of course.<P>I,m low tech here, I use a old half inch Main sheet off my sailboat. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Hank is Here||08-29-2001||05:29 PM||||On the rollbacks I have when I transport cars of any make, model or vintage I have lasso straps that fit around the wheels.  All the strap is is a normal cargo strap about 1.5 to 2 inches wide or so with a metal ring at the end.  run the strap through the ring, throw the large loop over the tire, get the rachet binder and tighten down.  I like this method since you are not pulling on the suspension at all, the force is at the wheels.   This is especially nice when hauling Caddies and Lincons with air-ride suspension  not to burn out their air pumps.     ||||reg||1
